In modern MSTest (3.x+), you can inject `TestContext` via the constructor and use `[ClassInitialize]`/`[TestInitialize]` with async support. Here's the recommended pattern:

```csharp
[TestClass]
public class MyTests
{
    private readonly TestContext _testContext;

    // Constructor injection for TestContext (MSTest 3.x+)
    public MyTests(TestContext testContext)
    {
        _testContext = testContext;
    }

    [ClassInitialize]
    public static async Task ClassSetup(TestContext context)
    {
        // One-time async setup for the entire class
        await Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    [TestInitialize]
    public async Task TestSetup()
    {
        // Per-test async initialization
        _testContext.WriteLine("Setting up test...");
        await Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    [TestMethod]
    public void SyncTest()
    {
        Assert.IsNotNull(_testContext);
    }

    [TestMethod]
    public async Task AsyncTest()
    {
        await Task.Delay(10);
        Assert.IsNotNull(_testContext);
    }

    [TestCleanup]
    public async Task TestTeardown()
    {
        await Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    [ClassCleanup]
    public static async Task ClassTeardown()
    {
        await Task.CompletedTask;
    }
}
```

**Key points:**

- **Constructor injection** of `TestContext` requires MSTest **3.x+** (you have 3.8.2 ✅)
- The older `public TestContext TestContext { get; set; }` property approach still works but constructor injection is preferred
- `[TestInitialize]` / `[TestCleanup]` can be `async Task`
- `[ClassInitialize]` must be `static` and takes a `TestContext` parameter
- Constructor runs before `[TestInitialize]`; use the constructor for sync setup and `[TestInitialize]` for async setup
